# Python conventions

- Type annotations on every public function; `Any` only with a comment saying why. The type checker in the stop-hook is the gate.
- Validate at the boundary (pydantic / serializers / dataclasses with validation); inside the module trust the types.
- No bare `except:`; catch the narrowest exception and re-raise with context (`raise ... from err`).
- Migrations (Alembic / Django) are human-only. If a task needs one, describe the schema change and stop.
- Settings come from one settings module reading the environment; no `os.environ` reads scattered through the code.
- Views / routes are thin: parse, call a service, map the result. Business logic lives in services / domain modules.
- Tests use pytest; fixtures over setup methods; no network, no real clock.
